                "target_scores": {
                    "The mass spectrometry data shows:\n- Base peak (100% intensity) at m/z 141.11536410745\n- Isotope peaks at m/z 142, 143, 144, 145, 146 with decreasing intensities\n\nThe base peak at m/z 141.115 likely represents the [M+H]+ ion (protonated molecular ion) in ESI positive mode, which is common for ESI-MS. This means the molecular weight would be approximately 141.115 - 1.007 = 140.108 Da.\n\nThe isotope pattern shows:\n- M+1 peak at ~9.23% (m/z 142)\n- M+2 peak at ~0.58% (m/z 143)\n\nLet me analyze what molecular formula could give a molecular weight of ~140.108:\n\nFor organic compounds with C, H, O, and possibly N:\n- The M+1 peak intensity of ~9.23% suggests approximately 8-9 carbon atoms (each carbon contributes ~1.1% due to 13C natural abundance)\n- The very small M+2 peak (~0.58%) is consistent with natural isotope distribution\n\nLet me consider possible formulas around mass 140.108:\n- C8H12O2: 140.0837 (too low)\n- C8H13NO: 139.0997 (close but has N)\n- C9H16O: 140.1201 (close)\n- C10H12: 132.0939 (too low)\n- C7H12O3: 144.0786 (too high)\n\nWait, let me recalculate more carefully. The exact mass is 140.108 (subtracting 1.007 for H+).\n\nActually, looking at common molecular formulas:\n- C8H12O2 = 8(12.000) + 12(1.008) + 2(15.999) = 96 + 12.096 + 31.998 = 140.094\n- C9H16O = 9(12.000) + 16(1.008) + 15.999 = 108 + 16.128 + 15.999 = 140.127\n- C10H20 = 10(12.000) + 20(1.008) = 120 + 20.16 = 140.16\n\nThe observed mass of 140.108 is closest to C8H12O2 (140.084 exact mass).\n\nLet me verify with the isotope pattern:\n- For C8H12O2: 8 carbons would give ~8.8% M+1 peak, which matches the observed 9.23% reasonably well.\n\nThe molecular formula is most likely C8H12O2.": 0.0,
                    "The mass spectrometry data shows:\n- Base peak at m/z 141.11536 (100% intensity)\n- Isotope peaks at m/z 142.119, 143.121, 144.123, 145.126, 146.129\n\nThe base peak at m/z 141.11536 likely represents [M+H]+ (protonated molecular ion) in ESI positive mode, which is common for ESI-MS.\n\nIf this is [M+H]+, then the molecular weight would be:\nM = 141.11536 - 1.00783 (mass of H+) = 140.10753\n\nNow I need to determine what molecular formula corresponds to a mass of approximately 140.108.\n\nLet me consider common organic compounds with this mass:\n- C7H12N2O: 7(12.000) + 12(1.008) + 2(14.003) + 1(15.999) = 84 + 12.096 + 28.006 + 15.999 = 140.101\n- C8H16O2: 8(12.000) + 16(1.008) + 2(15.999) = 96 + 16.128 + 31.998 = 144.126 (too high)\n- C6H12N2O2: 6(12.000) + 12(1.008) + 2(14.003) + 2(15.999) = 72 + 12.096 + 28.006 + 31.998 = 144.100 (too high)\n- C7H12N2O: 140.095 (close)\n- C8H12N2: 8(12.000) + 12(1.008) + 2(14.003) = 96 + 12.096 + 28.006 = 136.102 (too low)\n- C10H12O: 10(12.000) + 12(1.008) + 1(15.999) = 120 + 12.096 + 15.999 = 148.095 (too high)\n- C9H16O: 9(12.000) + 16(1.008) + 1(15.999) = 108 + 16.128 + 15.999 = 140.127 (close but slightly high)\n\nLet me calculate more precisely:\nC7H12N2O: 7(12.0000) + 12(1.00783) + 2(14.00307) + 15.99491 = 84.0000 + 12.09396 + 28.00614 + 15.99491 = 140.09501\n\nThis is very close to 140.10753. The difference is about 0.012, which is reasonable given measurement precision.\n\nLooking at the isotope pattern:\n- M+1 peak is 9.23% of base peak\n- This suggests presence of carbon atoms (each C contributes ~1.1% to M+1)\n- 9.23% / 1.1% ≈ 8-9 carbons, but C7H12N2O has 7 carbons which would give ~7.7%\n- With 2 nitrogens (each N contributes ~0.37%), that adds ~0.74%\n- Total: 7.7% + 0.74% = 8.44%, which is close to 9.23%\n\nC7H12N2O seems like the most likely formula.": 0.0,
                    "The mass spectrometry data shows:\n- Base peak (100% intensity) at m/z 141.11536410745\n- Isotope peaks at m/z 142, 143, 144, 145, 146 with decreasing intensities\n\nThe base peak at m/z 141.115 likely represents the base peak, which is common for ESI-MS. This means the molecular weight would be approximately 141.115 - 1.007 = 140.108 Da.\n\nThe isotope pattern shows:\n- M+1 peak at ~9.23% (m/z 142)\n- M+2 peak at ~0.58% (m/z 143)\n\nLet me analyze what molecular formula could give a molecular weight of ~140.108:\n\nFor organic compounds with C, H, O, and possibly N:\n- The M+1 peak intensity of ~9.23% suggests approximately 8-9 carbon atoms (each carbon contributes ~1.1% due to 13C natural abundance)\n- The very small M+2 peak (~0.58%) is consistent with natural isotope distribution\n\nLet me consider possible formulas around mass 140.108:\n- C8H15NO: 140.1151 (close)\n- C9H16O: 140.1201 (close)\n- C10H12: 132.0939 (too low)\n- C7H12O3: 144.0786 (too high)\n\nCalculating exact mass for C8H15NO:\nC8: 8 x 12.0000 = 96.0000\nH15: 15 x 1.00783 = 15.11745\nN: 14.00307\nO: 15.99491\nTotal = 96.0000 + 15.11745 + 14.00307 + 15.99491 = 141.11543 (which corresponds to the base peak)\n\nThe molecular formula is most likely C8H15NO.": 1.0,
