.1001 (too high)\n\nLet me try C8H12O2:\nC8: 8 x 12.0000 = 96.0000\nH12: 12 x 1.00783 = 12.09396\nO2: 2 x 15.99491 = 31.98982\nTotal = 96.0000 + 12.09396 + 31.98982 = 140.08378 (close!)\n\nLet me try C7H16O3:\nC7: 7 x 12.0000 = 84.0000\nH16: 16 x 1.00783 = 16.12528\nO3: 3 x 15.99491 = 47.97473\nTotal = 84.0000 + 16.12528 + 47.97473 = 148.10001 (too high)\n\nLet me try C8H16O:\nC8: 8 x 12.0000 = 96.0000\nH16: 16 x 1.00783 = 16.12528\nO: 15.99491\nTotal = 96.0000 + 16.12528 + 15.99491 = 128.12019 (too low)\n\nLet me try C9H16O:\nC9: 9 x 12.0000 = 108.0000\nH16: 16 x 1.00783 = 16.12528\nO: 15.99491\nTotal = 108.0000 + 16.12528 + 15.99491 = 140.12019 (very close!)\n\nLet me recalculate C9H16O more precisely:\nC9: 9 x 12.0000 = 108.0000\nH16: 16 x 1.007825 = 16.1252\nO: 15.994915\nTotal = 140.120115\n\nThis is very close to 140.10753. The difference might be due to rounding or measurement precision.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The exact mass for C9H16O should be:\n9 x 12.0000 + 16 x 1.007825 + 15.994915 = 140.120115\n\nBut our observed mass is 140.10753, which is about 0.013 lower.\n\nLet me try C8H12O2 again with exact masses:\n8 x 12.0000 + 12 x 1.007825 + 2 x 15.994915 = 140.08373\n\nThis is about 0.024 lower than observed.\n\nLet me try C10H12:\n10 x 12.0000 + 12 x 1.007825 = 132.09390 (too low)\n\nLet me try C10H16:\n10 x 12.0000 + 16 x 1.007825 = 136.1252 (too low)\n\nLet me try C9H12O:\n9 x 12.0000 + 12 x 1.007825 + 15.994915 = 136.088815 (too low)\n\nLet me try C10H12O:\n10 x 12.0000 + 12 x 1.007825 + 15.994915 = 148.088815 (too high)\n\nLet me reconsider C8H16O2:\n8 x 12.0000 + 16 x 1.007825 + 2 x 15.994915 = 144.11523\n\nThis is very close to 141.11536! Wait, that's almost exactly the observed m/z value.\n\nActually, if the observed m/z is 141.11536 and this represents [M+H]+, then M = 140.10753.\n\nBut if 141.11536 is actually [M+Na]+ (sodium adduct, also common in ESI), then:\nM = 141.11536 - 22.98977 = 118.12559\n\nLet me check formulas for mass 118:\nC6H14O2: 6 x 12 + 14 x 1.007825 + 2 x 15.994915 = 118.09938 (close!)\n\nActually, let me be more precise:\nC6H14O2: 72.0000 + 14.10955 + 31.98983 = 118.09938\n\nThe difference is 118.12559 - 118.09938 = 0.02621, which is significant.\n\nLet me try C7H14O:\n7 x 12 + 14 x 1.007825 + 15.994915 = 114.10447 (too low)\n\nLet me try C8H14:\n8 x 12 + 14 x 1.007825 = 110.10955 (too low)\n\nLet me try C8H18:\n8 x 12 + 18 x 1.007825 = 114.14085 (too low)\n\nLet me try C9H18:\n9 x 12 + 18 x 1.007825 = 126.14085 (too high)\n\nLet me try C7H18O:\n7 x 12 + 18 x 1.007825 + 15.994915 = 118.13577 (very close to 118.12559!)\n\nActually, the difference is only 0.01018, which could be within measurement error.\n\nBut wait, I should stick with the most likely interpretation: [M+H]+ = 141.11536, so M = 140.10753.\n\nThe closest match is C8H16O2 with exact mass 144.11523... no that's wrong.\n\nLet me recalculate C8H16O2:\n8 x 12.0000 = 96.0000\n16 x 1.007825 = 16.1252\n2 x 15.994915 = 31.98983\nTotal = 144.11503\n\nThat's too high by about 4 Da.\n\nGoing back to C9H16O:\n9 x 12.0000 = 108.0000\n16 x 1.007825 = 16.1252\n1 x 15.994915 = 15.994915\nTotal = 140.120115\n\nThis is 0.0126 higher than 140.10753.\n\nLet me try C10H12O:\n10 x 12.0000 = 120.0000\n12 x 1.007825 = 12.0939\n1 x 15.994915 = 15.994915\nTotal = 148.088815 (too high)\n\nLet me try C8H12O2:\n8 x 12.0000 = 96.0000\n12 x 1.007825 = 12.0939\n2 x 15.994915 = 31.98983\nTotal = 140.08373\n\nThis is 0.0238 lower than 140.10753.\n\nHmm, C9H16O at 140.120115 seems closest, being only 0.013 higher.\n\nBut let me also consider if there's nitrogen. C7H12N2O:\n7 x 12.0000 = 84.0000\n12 x 1.007825 = 12.0939\n2 x 14.003074 = 28.006148\n1 x 15.994915 = 15.994915\nTotal = 140.094963\n\nThis is 0.0126 lower than 140.10753.\n\nBoth C9H16O and C7H12N2O are about 0.013 away from the target, but in opposite directions.\n\nGiven the isotope pattern, I can check for nitrogen. The M+1 peak intensity is 9.23%, which for a compound with:\n- C9: 9 x 1.1%